t22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Exchange Contracts   $-                       $(4,584,788)                                   $-                                         $(4,584,788) \par Futures Contracts                     (7,719,076)              -                                              -                                          (7,719,076) \par Total Liabilities                     $(7,719,076)             $(4,584,788)                                   $-                                         $(12,303,864) \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amounts listed as "-" are $0 or round to $0.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the fiscal year ended October 31, 2022, there were no significant changes to the fair valuation methodologies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 b. Restricted Securities:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Restricted securities are privately-placed securities whose resale is restricted under U.S. securities laws. The Fund may invest in restricted securities, including unregistered securities eligible for resale without registration pursuant to Rule 144A and privately-placed securities of U.S. and non-U.S. issuers offered outside the U.S. without registration pursuant to Regulation S under the Securities Act of 1933, as amended. Rule 144A securities may be freely traded among certain qualified institutional investors, such as the Fund, but resale of such securities in the U.S. is permitted only in limited circumstances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 c. Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Translation:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign securities, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and other assets and liabilities denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are translated into U.S. Dollars at the exchange rate of said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the U.S. Dollar, as of the Valuation Time, as provided by an independent pricing service approved by the Board.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amounts are translated into U.S. Dollars on the following basis:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 i) market value of investment securities, other assets and liabilities - at the current daily rates of exchange at the Valuation Time; and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ii) purchases and sales of investment securities, income and expenses - at the relevant rates of exchange prevailing on the respective dates of such transactions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fund does not isolate that portion of gains and losses on investments in equity securities due to changes in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates from the portion due to changes in market prices of equity securities. Accordingly, realized and unrealized foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains and losses with respect to such securities are included in the reported net realized and unrealized gains and losses on investment transactions balances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 30 abrdn Asia-Pacific Income Fund, Inc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes to Financial Statements (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 31, 2022\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Net exchange gain/(loss) is realized from sales and maturities of portfolio securities, sales of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , settlement of securities transactions, dividends, interest and foreign withholding taxes recorded on the Fund's books. Net unrealized \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation/(depreciation) includes changes in the value of portfolio securities and other assets and liabilities arising as a result of changes in the exchange rate. The net realized and unrealized \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gain/(loss) shown in the composition of net assets represents \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gain/(loss) for book purposes that may not have been recognized for tax purposes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign security and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions may involve certain considerations and risks not typically associated with those of domestic origin, including unanticipated movements in the value of the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. Dollar. Generally, when the U.S. Dollar rises in value against foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Fund's investments denominated in that foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will lose value because the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is worth fewer U.S. Dollars; the opposite effect occurs if the U.S. Dollar falls in relative value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 d. Derivative Financial Instruments:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fund is authorized to use derivatives to manage \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk, interest rate risk and credit risk and to replicate, or use as a substitute for, physical securities. Losses may arise due to changes in the value of the contract or if the counterparty does not perform under the contract. The use of derivative instruments involves, to varying degrees, elements of market risk in excess of the amount recognized in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Forward Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Exchange Contracts\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A forward foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange contract ("forward contract") involves an obligation to purchase and sell a specific \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a future date, which may be any fixed number of days from the date of the contract agreed upon by the parties, at a price set at the time of the contract. Forward contracts are used to manage the Fund's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure in an efficient manner. They are used to sell unwanted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure that comes with holding securities in a market, or to buy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure where the exposure from holding securities is insufficient to give the desired \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure either in absolute terms or relative to the benchmark. The use of forward contracts allows the separation of decision-making between markets and their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The forward contract is marked-to-market daily and the change in market value is recorded by the Fund as unrealized appreciation or depreciation. Forward contracts' prices are received daily from an independent pricing provider. When the forward contract is closed, the Fund records a realized gain or loss equal to the difference between the value at the time it was opened and the value at the time it was closed. These realized and unrealized gains and losses are reported on the Statement of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Fund could be exposed to risks if the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 counterparties to the contracts are unable to meet the terms of their contracts or from unanticipated movements in exchange rates. These realized and unrealized gains and losses are reported on the Statements of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . During the fiscal year ended October 31, 2022, the Fund used forward contracts to hedge its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the Fund may enter into forward contracts to seek to reduce \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rate risks, transactions in such contracts involve certain risks. The Fund could be exposed to risks if the counterparties to the contracts are unable to meet the terms of their contracts and from unanticipated movements in exchange rates. Thus, while the Fund may benefit from such transactions, unanticipated changes in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prices may result in a poorer overall performance for the Fund than if it had not engaged in any such transactions. Moreover, there may be imperfect correlation between the Fund's portfolio holdings or securities quoted or denominated in a particular \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and forward contracts entered into by the Fund. Such imperfect correlation may prevent the Fund from achieving a complete hedge, which will expose the Fund to the risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loss.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Forward contracts are subject to the risk that a counterparty to such contracts may default on their obligations. Since a forward contract is not guaranteed by an exchange or clearing house, a default on the contract would deprive the Fund of unrealized profits, transaction costs or the benefits of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedge or force the Fund to cover its purchase or sale commitments, if any, at the market price at the time of the default.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Futures Contracts\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fund may invest in financial futures contracts ("futures contracts") for the purpose of hedging its existing portfolio securities, or securities that the Fund intends to purchase, against fluctuations in value caused by changes in prevailing market interest rates or prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Futures contracts may also be entered into for non-hedging purposes, however, in those instances, (a) either the aggregate initial margin and premiums required to establish the Fund's position may not exceed 5% of the Fund's NAV after taking into account unrealized profits and unrealized losses on any such contract into which it has entered into, or (b) the aggregate net notional value of the Fund's position may not exceed 100% of the Fund's NAV after taking into account unrealized profits and unrealized losses on any such contract which it has entered into.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Upon entering into a futures contract, the Fund is required to pledge to the broker an amount of cash and/or other assets equal to a certain percentage of the contract amount. This payment is known as initial margin. Subsequent payments, known as "variation margin," are calculated each day, depending on the daily fluctuations in the fair value/market value of the underlying assets. An unrealized gain/(loss) equal to the variation margin is recognized on a daily basis. When the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 abrdn Asia-Pacific Income Fund, Inc. 31\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes to Financial Statements (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 31, 2022\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 contract expires or is closed, the gain/(loss) is realized and is presented in the Statement of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a net realized gain/(loss) on futures contracts. Futures contracts are valued daily at their last quoted sale price on the exchange on which they are traded.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A "sale" of a futures contract means a contractual obligation to deliver the securities or foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  called for by the contract at a fixed price at a specified time in the future. A "purchase" of a futures contract means a contractual obligation to acquire the securities or foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a fixed price at a specified time in the future. During the year ended October 31, 2022, the Fund used U.S. Treasury futures to efficiently manage U.S. interest rate exposure and hedge the U.S. interest rate risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There are significant risks associated with the Fund's use of futures contracts, including the following: (1) the success of a hedging strategy may depend on the ability of the Fund's investment manager and/or sub-adviser to predict movements in the prices of individual securities, fluctuations in markets and movements in interest rates; (2) there may be an imperfect or no correlation between the movement in the price of futures contracts, interest rates and the value/market value of the securities held by the Fund; (3) there may not be a liquid secondary market for a futures contract; (4) trading restrictions or limitations may be imposed by an exchange; and (5) government regulations may restrict trading in futures contracts. In addition, should market conditions change unexpectedly, the Fund may not achieve the anticipated benefits of the futures contracts and may realize a loss.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Swaps\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A swap is an agreement that obligates two parties to exchange a series of cash flows and/or meet certain obligations at specified intervals based upon or calculated by reference to changes in specified prices or rates (interest rates in the case of interest rate swaps, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates in the case of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps) or the occurrence of a credit event with respect to an underlying reference obligation (in the case of a credit default swap) for a specified amount of an underlying asset or notional principal amount. The Fund will enter into swaps only on a net basis, which means that the two payment streams are netted out, with the Fund receiving or paying, as the case may be, only the amount of the difference between the two payments. Except for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps and credit default swaps, the notional principal amount is used solely to calculate the payment streams but is not exchanged. With respect to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps, actual principal amounts of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be exchanged by the counterparties at the initiation, and again upon the termination of the transaction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traditionally, swaps were customized, privately negotiated agreements executed between two parties ("OTC Swaps") but since 2013, certain swaps are required to be cleared pursuant to rules and regulations related to the Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act ("Dodd Frank") and/or Regulation (EU) No\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 648/2012 on OTC Derivatives, Central Counterparties and Trade Repositories ("EMIR") ("Cleared Swaps"). Like OTC Swaps, Cleared Swaps are negotiated bilaterally. Unlike OTC Swaps, the act of clearing results in two swaps executed between each of the parties and a central counterparty ("CCP"), and thus the counterparty credit exposure of the parties is to the CCP rather than to one another. Upon entering into a Cleared Swap, the Fund is required to pledge an amount of cash and/or other assets equal to a certain percentage of the contract amount. This payment is known as "initial margin". Subsequent payments, known as "variation margin," are calculated each day, depending on the daily fluctuations in the fair value/ market value of the underlying assets. An unrealized gain or loss equal to the variation margin is recognized on a daily basis. When the contract matures or is terminated, the gain or loss is realized and is presented in the Statements of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a net realized gain or loss on swap contracts. As of March 2017, the Fund may be required to provide variation and/or initial margin for OTC Swaps pursuant to further rules and regulations related to Dodd Frank and EMIR. The margin requirements associated with OTC Swaps and Cleared Swaps may not be the same.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rights and obligations of the parties to a swap are memorialized in either an International Swap Dealers Association, Inc. Master Agreement ("ISDA") for OTC Swaps or a futures agreement with an OTC addendum for Cleared Swaps ("Clearing Agreement"). These agreements are with certain counterparties whose creditworthiness is monitored on an ongoing basis by risk professionals. Both the ISDA and Clearing Agreement maintain provisions for general obligations, representations, agreements, collateral, and events of default or termination. The occurrence of a specified event of default or termination by one party may give the other party the right to terminate and settle all of its contracts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Entering into swap agreements involves, to varying degrees, elements of credit, market and interest risk in excess of the amounts reported on the Statement of Assets and Liabilities. Such risks involve the possibility that there will be no liquid market for these agreements, that the counterparty to the agreements may default on its obligation to perform and that there may be unfavorable changes in the value of the index or securities underlying the agreement. The Fund's maximum risk of loss from counterparty risk related to swaps is the fair value of the contract. This risk is mitigated by the posting of collateral by the counterparties to the Fund to cover the Fund's exposure to the counterparty.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Interest Rate Swaps\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fund may use interest rate swap contracts to manage its exposure to interest rates. Interest rate swap contracts typically represent the exchange between the Fund and a counterparty of respective commitments to make variable rate and fixed rate payments with\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 32 abrdn Asia-Pacific Income Fund, Inc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes to Financial Statements (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 31, 2022\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 respect to a notional amount of principal. Interest rate swap contracts may have a term that is greater than one year, but typically require periodic interim settlement in cash, at which time the specified value of the variable interest rate is reset for the next settlement period. Net\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 payments of interest are recorded as realized gains or losses. During the period that the swap contract is open, the contract is marked-to-market as the net amount due to or from the Fund and changes in the value of swap contracts are recorded as unrealized gains or losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Summary of Derivative Instruments:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fund may use derivatives for various purposes as noted above. The following is a summary of the fair value of derivative instruments, not accounted for as hedging instruments, as of October 31, 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                               Risk Exposure Category \par                                               InterestRateContracts   ForeignCurrencyContracts   CreditContracts   EquityContracts   CommodityContracts   Total \par                                              \par \par Assets: \par Unrealized appreciation on: \par Forward Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Exchange Contracts   $-                      $1,958,162                 $-                $-                $-                   $1,958,162 \par Futures Contracts                             6,212,432               -                          -                 -                 -                    6,212,432 \par Total                                         $6,212,432              $1,958,162           