\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  will not stop sharp declines, official warns\b0\par\par\uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par 07:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds context on Wednesday's rate check, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  report)\par \par \uc2 By Kaori Kaneko and Daniel Leussink\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 15 (Reuters) - Japan's efforts to stop the yen's sharp falls through unilateral market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only have a limited impact, a senior member of the country's ruling party warned, as data showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent tumble blowing the trade gap out to a record.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen held above 24-year lows against the U.S. dollar on Thursday, a day after authorities issued their clearest signal yet that they were not comfortable with recent sharp declines in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and were preparing for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen last traded around 143.62 per dollar, down about 0.3%, having shot up against the greenback on Wednesday on news of the possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Satsuki Katayama, the ruling Liberal Democratic Party's (LDP) head of a research commission on financial affairs, said Tokyo lacked effective means to combat the yen's falls and that unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be limited.\par \par \uc2 "Solo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be that effective" in stemming sharp yen falls, which are driven by the interest-rate gap between the United States and Japan, Katayama told Reuters in an interview on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Persistently high inflation in the United States and elsewhere has forced the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to aggressively raise interest rates, giving the dollar a significant yield advantage that has triggered a rampaging rally against its major global peers, including the yen.\par \par \uc2 Katayama, who is also a former \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official with financial markets expertise, said raising Japan's ultra-low interest rates would also be difficult given the impact that could have on the country's 550 trillion yen ($3.84 trillion) of bank loans.\par \par \uc2 That sentiment was echoed by \uc2 Yuichiro Tamaki\uc2 , the head of the opposition Democratic Party for the People, who said a rate hike would do more harm than good for the economy and instead called on more fiscal support.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan on Wednesday conducted a rate check with banks in apparent preparation to step in to reduce the yen's falls, which sent it up more than 1% and highlighted its nervousness about its steep declines.\par \par \uc2 WEAKER \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Japan's policymakers have historically favoured a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which makes exports more competitive, but now worry about the inflationary effect it will have on the cost of everything from food and dining out to utility and transport bills more expensive.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the rate check would give only brief support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as Tokyo could struggle to gain consent from \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  counterparts to conduct yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Other Asian economies are also turning their attention to risks from weaker domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In South Korea, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authority was seen selling dollars to curb the fall in the won, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit its lowest in nearly 13-1/2 years.\par \par \uc2 Separately, data on Thursday showed the trade deficit hit its biggest in a single month on record in August at $19.7 billion. The blowout follows a nearly 20% slide in the yen since the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 The weak yen caused particular pain for household budgets that are facing pressure from price rises, said Ayako Sera, market strategist at \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Trust Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "When comparing the positive and negative impact the weak yen is having on the situation in Japan overall, there are clearly more negatives," Sera said.\par \par \uc2 The trade data showed the average exchange rate was 135.08 yen per dollar, a 22.9% jump of the greenback against the yen from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kaori Kaneko, Daniel Leussink, Tetsushi Kajimoto and Takaya Yamaguchi; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-15T06:57:19.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220915ei9f00rgy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
