\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EXCLUSIVE-Bank of Korea's Rhee says policy tightening unlikely to end before Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 905 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2022\par 02:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats item first carried on Sunday.)\par \par \uc2 By Howard Schneider, Ann Saphir and Cynthia Kim\par \par \uc2 JACKSON HOLE, Wyo./SEOUL, Aug 28 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Korea (BOK) \uc2 must keep raising interest rates until the rate of inflation is in decline, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely could not halt its tightening before the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 , Governor Rhee Chang-yong said on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Reuters, Rhee also said \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to take steps, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the won against the dollar, if needed, should the bank determine speculative forces are causing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall.\par \par \uc2 Rhee's comments, on the sidelines of the Jackson Hole conference of central bankers in the U.S. state of Wyoming, dampened speculation that the \uc2 BOK\uc2  might be one of the first big central banks to ease off in the global battle against the steepest inflation in decades.\par \par \uc2 Asia's fourth-largest economy has been in the vanguard of global tightening. The \uc2 BOK\uc2  was among the first central banks to abandon pandemic-era monetary stimulus, raising its key policy rate by 2 percentage points since August last year to 2.5% .\par \par \uc2 Dollar appreciation driven by Fed rate increases has added to inflation in many open economies around the world, including South Korea, as local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall in value.\par \par \uc2 "We are now independent from government, but we are not independent from the Fed," Rhee said. "So if the Fed continues to increase the interest rate, it will have a depreciation pressure for our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Although the \uc2 BOK\uc2  began raising interest rates before the Fed, with its first hike a year ago, "whether we can end earlier \u8211\'20\'13 I don\u8217\'20\'19t think so."\par \par \uc2 South Korea's inflation is largely the result of external issues such as energy prices, Rhee said.\par \par \uc2 "If you ask me, whether I'm going to stop... what happens if the oil price increases again?" he said. "It's very hard for us to know the exact timing, given the importance of the external shock."\par \par \uc2 Even though he expects domestic inflation to slow in August compared with the 6.3% rate seen in July, it is "too premature" to say it has peaked, especially since, as winter approaches, gas prices could again rise.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOK\uc2  raised rates by a quarter point at its last meeting and said further quarter-point increases "will be appropriate for some time as long as inflation paths remain as currently presumed."\par \par \uc2 At this point, "I cannot say we are ahead of the curve," Rhee said. "As long as inflation remains high, meaning 4%-5%... then we will definitely continue to emphasize the normalization" of interest rates.\par \par \uc2 EYE ON THE WON\par \par \uc2 Inflation in South Korea is forecast around 5% by the end of 2022, and to fall through 2023. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like many others, targets 2% inflation.\par \par \uc2 At Jackson Hole, central bankers used largely the same language to describe their battle against rising prices. Though the headline problem is the same - inflation far above their established targets - the sources of price pressure and therefore the policy responses differ among countries.\par \par \uc2 For smaller, open economies like South Korea's, the situation is particularly complex because of the spillover effects from policies set elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  on Friday kicked off the Jackson Hole conference by saying the Fed will raise rates as high as needed to restrict growth, and would keep them there "for some time" to bring down inflation.\par \par \uc2 His speech sparked a sell-off in U.S. equity markets, and Rhee said on Monday attention would turn to the won.\par \par \uc2 The won, one of Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has dropped about 11% against the dollar this year, and local officials have stepped up surveillance of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movements.\par \par \uc2 Rhee said so far he did not see the depreciation as driven by speculation or South Korea's economic fundamentals, but as part of the dollar's rising global strength.\par \par \uc2 "There are a few days we see movement that's too excessive - but so far I think our exchange rate movement is very much in line with major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Rhee said.\par \par \uc2 But should the \uc2 BOK\uc2  detect speculative moves in dollar-won trading, he stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The won has been falling faster than \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in neighboring China and Japan, partly because they maintain loose monetary policies, he said.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers from President \uc2 Yoon Suk\uc2 -yeol to Finance Minister \uc2 Choo Kyung\uc2 -ho have stepped up their rhetoric to try to slow the won's decline repeatedly over the last week.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Han Duck-soo said on Sunday the won's weakness should help South Korea's economy, in aspects of exports and the current account, adding that he hopes monetary policy may not have to be tightened by as much or as quickly as in the United States.\par \par \uc2 "This depreciation pressure due to the dollar strength actually is a bad factor for our inflation, because our imported prices increase a lot," Rhee said. But "the current depreciation pressure does not mean any liquidity problems or solvency problems, or credit problem for Korea." (Reporting by Howard Schneider and Ann Saphir in Jackson Hole, and Cynthia Kim in Seoul; Additional reporting by Jihoon Lee; Editing by William Mallard and Christopher Cushing)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-1T00:43:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220829ei8t002p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
