\par\par \b \uc2 Swap lines no fix for emerging markets trounced by strong dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 06:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, March 20 (Reuters) - Persistent weakness in emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday and enduring stresses in dollar funding markets showed that a spate of hurried swap lines between central banks had done little to alleviate the credit strains at the heart of the problem.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the South Korean won came off their lows but very slightly after the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  signed swaps with the central banks of Australia, Brazil, South Korea, Mexico, Singapore, Sweden, Denmark, Norway and New Zealand to allow them to tap up to $450 billion.               \par \par \uc2 That money is meant to help markets scrambling to get their hands on U.S. dollars because of worries about how badly the coronavirus pandemic will hit businesses and the world economy.               \par \par \uc2 "More dollar swap lines with central banks will be needed before the global economy stabilizes, but some point, there will be enough dollar supply to calm the markets," said Montreal-based Mathieu Savary, a strategist at BCA Research.               \par \par \uc2 "Gold prices are an indication that we are not there yet in terms of sufficient dollar liquidity."               \par \par \uc2 Prices of safe-haven gold are down more than 3% this week as investors rushed to dump the most liquid assets in a bid to raise cash.               \par \par \uc2 Dollar funding remained at a premium, with investors paying 169 basis points (bps) over interbank rates to swap 3-month Korean won into dollars, nearly three times the level last month.               \par \par \uc2 They were paying 46 bps more than interbank rates to swap euros into dollars on Friday, half the level early this week but still three times the average premium last month.               \par \par \uc2 The won is off the 2008 lows it hit on Thursday but still down 7% in two weeks.               \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps had managed to get more dollars into the banking system but the flow of that cash into brokerages, companies and other end users of dollar funding was still hamstrung by concerns over credit quality, analysts said.               \par \par \uc2 And those would persist until wider concerns over economic activity, which has ground to a near halt on account of the virus, abated.               \par \par \uc2 "While the additional swap lines announced by the Fed today seem to have given respite to a few embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they may not be sufficient to stop the dollar in its tracks," Oliver Allen of \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  wrote, terming the swaps as "a necessary, but not sufficient, condition to turn the tide."               \par \par \uc2 "A sustained retreat of the dollar may have to wait until the coronavirus outbreak shows clear signs of fading."               \par \par \uc2 Thursday's swaps were in addition to the permanent swaps the Fed has with central banks in Canada, the United Kingdom, Japan, Switzerland and with the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  provided $45 million of liquidity to foreign central banks in the latest week via its swap lines, the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  said on Thursday.               \par \par \uc2 The rush for dollar funding has pushed the dollar up 4% this month against a basket of peers, and sent almost every other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reeling.                              \par \par \uc2 The biggest losers in emerging markets have been the higher-yielding ones sought after by portfolio managers with heavy amounts of cheap dollar funding.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar is up nearly 16% this month versus the Russian rouble and 18% against the Mexican peso. Others such as the Indonesian rupiah, Turkish lira and South African rand also have suffered big losses.               \par \par \uc2 Central banks in Indonesia, India, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , New Zealand, Russia, Brazil and other places have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , offered dollar funding, or both. Denmark raised rates to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as other central banks cut them to bankroll businesses impacted by the pandemic.                              \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-3-20T06:39:05.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200320eg3k00sjt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
