\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-FACTBOX-Foreign funds flee Asia's emerging markets\b0\par\par\uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 16:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Refiles to add dropped word in second paragraph)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, March 2 (Reuters) - The coronavirus epidemic has spread from China to more than 50 countries, raising fears of a pandemic and global recession and sparking a flight by foreign funds out of Asia's emerging markets, from \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 Preliminary data from the Institute of International Finance (IIF) released on Monday showed portfolio managers pulled $9.7 billion from emerging markets stocks, predominantly from Asia.\par \par \uc2 Here is the latest snapshot of outflows and measures in response:\par \par \uc2 INDONESIA\par \par \uc2 Non-resident investors sold 19.36 trillion rupiah ($1.4 billion) of government bonds over the first four days of last week, according to government data. In equities, foreigners sold a net 4.16 trillion rupiah, according to Refinitiv Eikon data.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's rupiah has fallen more than 5% in two weeks. The yield on 10-year Indonesian sovereign bonds has jumped 50 basis points in four trading sessions .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday said it would cut banks' reserve requirements for savings in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 400 basis points to 4%, freeing $3.2 billion, and increase its own \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SOUTH KOREA\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Last week, foreign investors sold a net 3.6 trillion won ($3 billion) worth of stocks listed on the KOSPI and KOSDAQ indexes, the biggest outflow since the 2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The won has dropped 3.3% against the dollar since January.\par \par \uc2 The vice finance minister has vowed to use "all possible measures" to stabilise markets, though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held interest rates steady last week and stressed the limits of monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 MALAYSIA\par \par \uc2 As domestic political turmoil also hurt sentiment, foreign investors dumped 1.26 billion ringgit ($300 million) worth of Malaysian equities last week, the largest weekly outflow in 20 months.\par \par \uc2 For February, foreigners liquidated nearly 2 billion ringgit in stocks, the highest such outflow since August. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 3.4% against the dollar since Jan. 20.\par \par \uc2 TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors sold T$94.4 billion ($3.14 billion) in Taiwan stocks last week, the third worst weekly performance in history. The benchmark index shed 3.4% last week.\par \par \uc2 THAILAND\par \par \uc2 Foreigners liquidated 17.9 billion baht ($572 million) in bonds last week, the biggest selldown in six months. It was also the seventh straight week of foreigners selling stocks, with a net 4.2 billion baht outflow.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been among the worst performing in Asia this year, dropping almost 5% against the dollar. ($1 = 14,260 rupiah) ($1 = 4.2000 ringgit) ($1 = 30.0370 Taiwan dollars) ($1 = 31.3000 baht) ($1 = 1,196.4000 won) (Reporting by Liz Lee in Kuala Lumpur, Orathai Sriring in Bangkok, Cynthia Kim in Seoul, Gayatri Suroyo and Fransiska Nangoy in Jakarta and Emily Chan in Taipei Writing by Tom Westbrook Editing by Jacqueline Wong, Robert Birsel) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-3-2T16:02:29.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200302eg3201vz9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
