\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  Won Extends Fall on Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Speculation -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 195 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2016\par 05:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  won is extending its losses to the U.S. dollar, led by a weakening of the Japanese yen amid speculation that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  may be about to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. Bank of Japan Governor Haruhiko Kuroda, after a meeting with Prime Minister Abe Friday, said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is watching the yen closely. Though there was no mention of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the safe haven yen, which strengthened as much as 5.3% this week due to risk aversion, the news of a possible emergency BOJ meeting has stirred optimism for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. If the BOJ unleashes its reserves to buy U.S. dollars, so as to weaken the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the South Korea won, and even the Singapore dollar, will be dragged along. USD/KRW is now 1,210.5 from Thursday's close of 1,202.5, representing a 0.7% weakening of the won versus the dollar. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 11, 2016 23:57 ET (04:57 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160212ec2c000ae\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
