\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ringgit leads Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  gains on oil, BOJ's surprise minus interest rates\b0\par\par\uc2 942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2016\par 07:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Ringgit at 3-1/2-mth high as long-term foreigners buy\par     * Taiwan dollar up on exporters, foreigners; GDP ignored\par     * Won up on suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after dip on BOJ move\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Jan 29 (Reuters) - Malaysia's ringgit hit a\par 3-1/2-month high on Friday, leading gains among emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's surprise easing and higher\par oil prices.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  earlier added negative interest rates on\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deposits to its massive asset-buying programme,\par stunning financial markets that expected no action or a moderate\par increase in asset purchases. \par     The move helped Asian equity markets extend gains, while the\par yen lost 2 percent.\par     Such easing could help investors seek higher yields in\par emerging Asia, while the regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may not fully\par benefit given a slowing global economy, some analysts said.\par Regional units gave up some of initial gains after the BOJ's\par decision, with the South Korean won briefly turning\par weaker.\par     "The surprise BOJ move, along with expectations of \f28 \uc2 \f2 the ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's\par easing in March, will support risk assets and attract more carry\par trades into Asia," said Yuna Park, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and bond analyst at\par Dongbu Securities in Seoul, referring to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "Still, that is not necessarily seen only positive to Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as such easing could spur other central banks in\par emerging markets to cut interest rates."\par     South Korea's treasury bond futures rallied as\par foreign investors rushed to buy after the BOJ's move, although\par many of the analysts still see interest rates on hold for a\par prolonged period of time.\par     Some of central banks in emerging Asia already eased\par monetary policies or injected money to support growth and\par inflation despite worries about capital outflows due to\par expectations of U.S. interest rate hikes.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  continued to pump massive amounts\par of short-term liquidity into markets. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week cut the statutory reserve requirement\par ratio to add more liquidity. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Jan.\par 14 cut interest rates. \par     \par     OIL SHINES RINGGIT    \par     The ringgit jumped as much as 2.1 percent to 4.1200\par per dollar, its strongest since Oct. 15. Long-term foreign\par investors chased the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as higher crude prices eased\par concerns over Malaysia's falling oil and gas revenues.\par     Global benchmark Brent oil futures moved nearly 6.5\par percent higher so far this week on hopes of a deal among\par oil-producing countries to tackle a growing supply glut. \par     So far this week, the ringgit has advanced 4.2 percent\par against the dollar, helping it report a more than 4 percent gain\par in January, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomson Reuters\f28 \uc2 \f2  data. The appreciation\par would be the largest monthly rise in four years, the data\par showed.\par     "There has been a wave of aggressive USD selling seen from\par real money and while traders had anticipated good demand on\par dips, the support levels quickly evaporated when oil prices\par continued firming overnight," said Stephen Innes, senior trader\par for OANDA in Singapore.\par     "The downside (in dollar/ringgit) is looking very\par susceptible as the long USD/Asia liquidation continues," Innes\par said.\par     Adding to support on the ringgit, the government on Thursday\par maintained this year's fiscal deficit target at 3.1 percent of\par gross domestic product, defying expectations of a wider gap.\par  \par     The government's 10-year bond prices on Friday rose with the\par yield down to 3.857 percent, the lowest since May 6\par 2015.\par     Most regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose on Friday to report weekly\par gains, although they were still on the course for monthly\par losses.\par     The Taiwan dollar gained on exporter bids for\par month-end settlements, while foreign financial institutions\par bought the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with local stocks up.\par     Still, caution grew over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb further appreciation as data showed the\par island's economy grew in 2015 at the slowest pace since the\par global financial crisis. \par     The won gained as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities\par were suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem losses in the second-worst\par performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year. Exporters also\par bought it for month-end settlements.\par     The Singapore dollar advanced on the Chinese yuan's\par  stability. \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixed its daily\par guidance rates stronger over the past week. \par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0605 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       120.52         118.83       -1.40\par   Sing dlr        1.4216         1.4278       +0.44\par   Taiwan dlr      33.390         33.743       +1.06\par   Korean won     1197.72        1208.50       +0.90\par   Baht             35.73          35.82       +0.25\par   Peso             47.64          47.73       +0.19\par   Rupiah        13780.00       13870.00       +0.65\par   Rupee            67.88          68.23       +0.52\par   Ringgit         4.1200         4.2070       +2.11\par   Yuan            6.5760         6.5754       -0.01\par  \par   Change so far in 2016\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       120.52         120.30       -0.18\par   Sing dlr        1.4216         1.4177       -0.27\par   Taiwan dlr      33.390         33.066       -0.97\par   Korean won     1197.72        1172.50       -2.11\par   Baht             35.73          36.00       +0.76\par   Peso             47.64          47.06       -1.22\par   Rupiah        13780.00       13785.00       +0.04\par   Rupee            67.88          66.15       -2.55\par   Ringgit         4.1200         4.2935       +4.21\par   Yuan            6.5760         6.4936       -1.25\par  \par  (Additional reporting by Liang-Sa Loh in TAIPEI and Yena Park\par in SEOUL; Editing by Gopakumar Warrier)\f28 \par \par \uc2