\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Strong risk appetite, China service activity support Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2015\par 06:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Rupiah, Taiwan dollar near 2-week high on stocks\par     * Won around 3-month peak vs yen; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  caution grows\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Nov 4 (Reuters) - Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose\par on Wednesday as risk sentiment improved in the light of a\par probable increase in euro zone stimulus and a solid services\par survey from China that assuaged fears of a deepening economic\par slowdown. \par     The Indonesian rupiah hit a near two-week high as\par local stocks continued to rally ahead of third-quarter\par growth data that is expected to be slightly better than the\par previous three months. \par     The Taiwan dollar also reached its strongest level\par in about two weeks on firm domestic shares.\par     South Korea's won gained versus the dollar on\par exporter settlement demand. The won touched a near three-month\par peak.\par     The Malaysian ringgit advanced in thin trade as\par overnight rallies in crude prices alleviated concerns over the\par country's falling gas and oil revenues.\par     European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  reiterated that\par further easing was on the table for December. \par     "The prospect of more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) stimulus has\par investors chasing riskier assets and yield," says Stephen Innes,\par Senior Trader at \f28 \uc2 \f2 OANDA\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore.\par     Activity in China's services sector expanded at its fastest\par pace in three months in October on stronger new business, a\par private survey showed. \par     Still, investors remained wary ahead of October U.S. jobs\par data due on Friday, which will inform the Federal Reserve's\par inclination to raise interest rates in December.\par     Economists expect U.S. employers to have added 180,000 jobs\par last month, according to a Reuters poll.\par     \par     RUPIAH\par     The rupiah rose nearly 1 percent to 13,420 per dollar, its\par strongest since Oct 23. Jakarta shares advanced 1.5\par percent, outperforming most Southeast Asia peers.\par     Indonesia will announce economic growth data on Thursday.\par Growth in Southeast Asia's largest economy was expected to \par rebound in the third quarter to 4.79 percent on an annual basis\par from a 4.67 percent in the second quarter, a Reuters poll\par showed. \par     Most of the rupiah's non-deliverable forwards (NDFs)\par  gained on demand from leveraged funds. Most\par government bond prices rose.\par     The strength in NDFs led local investors to unwind bearish\par bets in spot rupiah.\par     Still, the rupiah has chart resistance at 13,392, its a\par 200-day moving average, analysts said. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been\par closing daily sessions weaker than the average since September\par 2014.\par     \par     TAIWAN DOLLAR\par     The Taiwan dollar advanced 0.9 percent to 32.320 per the\par U.S. dollar, its strongest since Oct. 23. Stocks jumped 1.6\par percent.\par     Foreign investors were net buyers in the market during the\par previous two consecutive days, purchasing a combined net T$14.5\par billion ($448.4 million), the Taiwan Stock Exchange data showed.\par  \par     \par     WON\par     The won gained as much as 0.4 percent to 9.2900 per the yen,\par its strongest since Aug 11.\par     Caution grew over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities to stem the won's strength versus\par the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The two countries compete for sales of various products such\par as cars.\par     "The yen/won found support around 9.3 as that was an\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  level before," said a foreign bank trader in Seoul.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0450 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       121.18         121.05       -0.11\par   Sing dlr        1.3952         1.3954       +0.01\par   Taiwan dlr      32.335         32.625       +0.90\par   Korean won     1128.90        1133.10       +0.37\par   Baht             35.50          35.57       +0.20\par   Peso             46.74          46.82       +0.16\par   Rupiah        13490.00       13550.00       +0.44\par   Rupee            65.57          65.65       +0.12\par   Ringgit         4.2650         4.2855       +0.48\par   Yuan            6.3340         6.3366       +0.04\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       121.18         119.66       -1.26\par   Sing dlr        1.3952         1.3260       -4.96\par   Taiwan dlr      32.335         31.718       -1.91\par   Korean won     1128.90        1099.30       -2.62\par   Baht             35.50          32.90       -7.32\par   Peso             46.74          44.72       -4.32\par   Rupiah        13490.00       12380.00       -8.23\par   Rupee  