\par\par \b \uc2 Australia Shares Pulled Lower by Resources Counters -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2015\par 04:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Australia's equities market is in the red, dragged down by a fall in resources stocks after a slide in oil prices and amid concerns about China's appetite for commodities. The \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 is down 0.4% at 5250.2, after ending the previous session flat despite China recording the slowest pace of growth since 2009 in the third quarter. The basket of energy shares is 3% lower, while the materials subindex is down 2%. By lunchtime, 814.7 million shares had been traded worth A$1.6 billion, Commonwealth Securities says. Newcrest (NCM.AU) is down 5.1% after reporting a drop in quarterly production over the prior three months. \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  (BHP.AU) and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) are 2.8% and 2.1% lower, respectively. Among energy shares, Woodside (WPL.AU) is down 4.1% and Santos (STO.AU) is 4.8% weaker, while \uc2 Oil Search\uc2  (OSH.AU) is off 1.6% after turning in quarterly production figures. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter : @RobbMStewart) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Speculation over how much more capital Australia's big banks need isn't likely to fade after the federal government embraces almost all the recommendations put forward in a sweeping financial system review almost a year ago. In particular, the prime minister and treasure support the view that capital adequacy ratios should be "unquestionably strong." What is unclear is just how much capital that will take, and when that will need to be achieved if larger capital buffers are deemed necessary by regulators. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; @RobbMStewart) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0212 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The latest \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  report on exchange rates is seen as being much toned down in its criticism of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, says Hong Seok-chan, a Seoul-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst of Daishi Economic Research Institute. "The Treasury seems to soften its critical stance on Korea, which may lighten the burden of financial authorities in Seoul," says Hong. Korea's finance ministry and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  both declined to comment as usual. "Korea continued to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to resist pressure for the won to appreciate against the dollar in the first half of 2015 but then sold \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in July and August to limit won depreciation, so that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the calendar year to date appears roughly balanced," said the U.S. semi-annual report on exchange rates. The report still urged Korea to refrain from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and also called for more transparency in Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy.(kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0205 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has given no signals in its October 6 policy meeting minutes that it is worried about the growth or inflation outlook, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Compared with the more dovish minutes of last month, there was an absence of concerns about slowing global growth and market volatility impacting local activity and confidence. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0204 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Shipping the first natural gas from Santos's (STO.AU) flagship GLNG project on Australia's east coast is a somewhat pyrrhic victory for the company's shareholders, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  reckons. For starters, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  disputes Santos's assertion the project was achieved within budget, with the brokerage pointing to an early US$2.5 billion cost "acceleration" along with about A$2.5 billion in "non-project" capital expenditure that leaves its model looking decidedly over budget. As well, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  notes big misses on reserves for the project versus expectations. "What should have been a defining moment for a company is a somewhat damp squib." It has a neutral stance and A$5.60 target on the shares, which are now down 4.2% at A$5.23. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter : @RobbMStewart) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0203 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] NZD/USD Intraday: Choppy. The pair remains in consolidation within the range between 0.6840 and 0.6780. The intraday volatility decreased sharply, as the Bollinger Bands are tightening, which also indicates that a breakout may occur in the near future. Furthermore, the intraday RSI is mixed to bearish below its neutrality area at 50. Hence, as long as 0.6840 is not surpassed, look for choppy price action with a bearish bias. A break below 0.6780 would trigger a bearish acceleration towards 0.6740. Alternatively, above 0.6840 look for further upside with 0.6900 & 0.6925 as targets. Trading Central (tradingcentral.com) is a commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0202 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/GBP Intraday: Downside prevails. The pair has accelerated to the downside after breaking below its previous support at 0.7345, and currently is capped by its descending 50-period intraday MA. The intraday RSI is around 50 and lacks upward momentum. A first target to the downside is therefore set at 0.7305. A break below this level would open the way to further weakness towards the horizontal support and overlap at 0.7270 in extension. Only a break above the key resistance at 0.7345 would call for further upside towards 0.7375 at first and then towards 0.7395 in extension. Trading Central (tradingcentral.com) is a commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0200 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Australia's equity market looks wedged between a weak global backdrop and a domestic picture that's likely to look worse in six months time, Macquarie reckons. The brokerage believes the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  200 remains in a 4950-5350 range, with the potential it could retest lows since there's not a lot of cushion in the valuation for earnings disappointment. And while there is the potential for a more sustained fourth-quarter rally, it recommends hedging this risk rather than positioning for it, with investor portfolios remaining defensive. It is neutral on the energy and materials sectors, and is using bank stocks as a "tactical level." Macquarie remains overweight growth and global industrials, offset by an underweight stance on domestic industrial stocks. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  200 currently is down 0.4% at 5246.5. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter : @RobbMStewart) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0157 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/JPY Intraday: Key resistance at 136.00. The pair remains under pressure below its key resistance at 136.00. The descending 50-period intraday MA maintains a bearish bias. And the intraday RSI lacks strong upward momentum. A first target to the downside is therefore set at the horizontal support and overlap at 134.75. A break below this level would open the way to further weakness towards 134.20 in extension. Only a break above the key resistance at 136.00 would call for further upside towards the horizontal resistance at 136.70 at first and then towards 137.00 in extension. Trading Central (tradingcentral.com) is a commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0156 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CAD Intraday: Upside prevails. The pair stands above its 20-period and 50-period intraday MAs and remains on the upside. The intraday RSI is well directed and calls for further upside as well. Further upside is therefore expected with the next horizontal resistance and overlap set at 1.3040 at first. A break above this level would call for further advance towards 1.3080 in extension. Only a break below the horizontal support at 1.2950 would open the way to further weakness towards 1.2900 at first. A second alternative target is set at 1.2850 in extension. Trading Central (tradingcentral.com) is a commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0156 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has re-iterated its neutral policy stance in the minutes of its most recent board meeting, leaving nothing to mount a case for a November rate cut, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  . While the overall tone remains neutral, the October RBA Board minutes have introduced a positive 'tweak' to the bank's discussion of employment conditions, while highlighting risks around developments in local property markets. "As such, there is nothing here to mount a case for a November rate cut, in our view. We remain comfortable with our view that the cash rate is likely to remain at 2.0% for some time," DB says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0155 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CHF Intraday: Upside prevails. The pair is trading on the upside while being supported by the rising 50-period intraday MA. The process of higher highs and lows remains intact, which should confirm a positive outlook. Besides, the intraday RSI stays above its neutrality level at 50. In these perspectives, as long as 0.9520 (our trailing stop loss) is not broken, look for a new rise to 0.9585 & 0.9615 in extension. Alternatively, below 0.9520 look for further downside with 0.9500 & 0.9475 as targets. Trading Central (tradingcentral.com) is a commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 19, 2015 22:20 ET (02:20 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151020ebak0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
