\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Seen Toning Down Criticism of South Korea -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2015\par 04:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The latest \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  report on exchange rates is seen as being much toned down in its criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, says Hong Seok-chan, a Seoul-based \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst of Daishi Economic Research Institute. "The Treasury seems to soften its critical stance on Korea, which may lighten the burden of financial authorities in Seoul," says Hong. Korea's finance ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  both declined to comment as usual. "Korea continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to resist pressure for the won to appreciate against the dollar in the first half of 2015 but then sold \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July and August to limit won depreciation, so that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the calendar year to date appears roughly balanced," said the U.S. semi-annual report on exchange rates. The report still urged Korea to refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and also called for more transparency in Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.(kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 19, 2015 22:12 ET (02:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151020ebak0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picw