\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Most Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  down, but China Q3 GDP number cuts losses\b0\par\par\uc2 636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2015\par 08:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Malaysia lawmaker seeks no-confidence vote against PM\par     * Won changes course hits 3-1/2 high after China Q3 GDP data\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par eased on Monday as China reported its weakest quarterly economic\par growth since the global financial crisis, while reduced fears of\par a hard-landing in the world's second-largest economy limited\par regional losses.\par     Malaysia's ringgit led regional depreciation as an\par opposition lawmaker submitted notice for a motion of no\par confidence against embattled Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Najib Razak\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The\par motion is unlikely to succeed. \par     The Chinese yuan slipped in line with a weaker\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  midpoint, although the economy's tepid but slightly\par higher-than-expected third quarter growth capped the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par slide. \par     Regional units started the day weaker against the dollar\par  after stronger-than-expected U.S. data kept alive\par prospects of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  hiking interest rates within\par this year.\par     The Chinese economy grew 6.9 percent in the third quarter\par from a year earlier, data showed, beating a market forecast of\par 6.8 percent. \par     Some emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut losses on the data, while\par the South Korean won reversed its slide and hit a\par 3-1/2-month high.\par     "China's slightly better-than-expected GDP data helped to\par soothe concerns over the extent of the slowdown," said Khoon\par Goh, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for ANZ in Singapore.\par     "However, I do not expect to see Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains extend\par too far as there are still downside growth risks in the region,\par and the poor export outlook will ultimately weigh on the\par region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par     \par     WON\par     The won rose as much as 0.8 percent to 1,120.6 per dollar,\par its strongest since July 3.\par     The South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  opened the local market softer as\par traders had raised bets on weaker economic growth in China, the\par largest market for South Korea's exports.\par     The stronger-than-expected China growth number, however,\par prompted traders to unwind those bearish positions on the won.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities were suspected of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the won's strength beyond 1,120, traders\par said.\par     That came as the won breached a chart resistance of a\par 200-day moving average at 1,124.3, analysts said. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par had been weaker than the average since early May.\par     The won briefly broke through another technical resistance\par at 1,120.9, the 61.8 percent Fibonacci retracement of its\par depreciation from April to September, but ended local trade\par weaker than that level. \par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLA