\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Global stock rallies support Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2015\par 08:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Won gains as Seoul shares up nearly 3 pct\par     * Ringgit rises on commodities\par     * Singapore dollar up on stop-loss U.S. dlr selling\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Sept 9 - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose on\par Wednesday as sharp rebounds in global equity markets boosted\par risk sentiment, and  on hopes of more stimulus by China.\par     The won rose as Seoul shares rallied and\par on speculation that the dollar buying linked to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tesco\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \par sale of its South Korean arm for $6.1 billion may have been\par completed.\par     Malaysia's ringgit rebounded in thin trading as\par higher commodity prices eased concerns over the country's\par exports.\par     Asia-Pacific stocks outside Japan jumped\par nearly 3 percent, tracking rallies in U.S. and European peers.\par     Chinese shares also gained more than 1 percent on growing\par expectations that Beijing would take additional steps to prevent\par a hard landing for the economy. \par     Worries about China's economy and the recent financial\par market rout sent emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to multi-year lows\par with the ringgit and Indonesian rupiah hitting their\par weakest levels since the Asian financial crisis 17 years ago.\par     "Today's rebounds (in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stocks) may be\par meaningful as they came after deep turmoil," said Jeong\par My-young, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung\f28 \uc2 \f2  Futures' research head in Seoul.\par     "We need to see how Chinese authorities handle the sluggish\par economy and markets for further improvement."\par     China's yuan turned weaker as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par was seen to be less aggressively \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     \par     WON\par     The won advanced as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's main stock index \par jumped 2.8 percent.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders also cut dollar holdings which they had\par built up on expectations of rising dollar demand from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tesco\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par deal.\par     The British supermarket said on Monday it was selling its\par South Korean arm to a group led by Seoul-based private equity\par firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 MBK Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par     The won's upside was limited as foreign investors continued\par to sell Seoul shares.\par     Foreigners were net sellers for a 25th consecutive session,\par dumping around 5 trillion won ($4.2 billion) worth of equities\par in total during the period, Korea Exchange data showed.\par     Earlier, the vice finance minister said South Korea is\par facing growing uncertainties as risks from China and the United\par States and a prolonged slump in exports threatens the economy.\par  \par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit gained as local stocks and most\par Malaysian government bond prices rose.\par     Despite improving global risk appetite, traders hesitated to\par buy the ringgit, given fragile sentiment on the country because\par of a corruption scandal swirling round Prime Minister Najib\par Razak and indebted state fund \f28 \uc2 \f2 1Malaysia Development Berhad\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     A senior Malaysian bank trader also doubted there would be\par any improvement in risk sentiment before the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal\par Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policy meeting next week.\par     "Money is flowing back to developed markets from emerging\par markets. How can we stop that?" said the trader.\par     \par     SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par     The Singapore dollar rose on stop-loss selling of the U.S.\par dollar around 1.4150 and 1.4140.\par     Still, some macro funds sold the Singapore dollar around its\par session high on concerns over a slowdown in the local economy.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0600 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       120.44         119.81       -0.52\par   Sing dlr        1.4119         1.4177       +0.41\par   Taiwan dlr      32.535         33.003       +1.44\par   Korean won     1188.52        1200.90       +1.04\par   Baht             36.01          36.17       +0.44\par   Peso             46.83          46.93       +0.21\par   Rupiah        14265.00       14275.00       +0.07\par   Rupee            66.34          66.55       +0.32\par   Ringgit         4.3210         4.3385       +0.40\par   Yuan            6.3730         6.3672       -0.09\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       120.44         119.66       -0.65\par   Sing dlr        1.4119         1.3260       -6.08\par   Taiwan dlr      32.535         31.718       -2.51\par   Korean won     1188.52        1099.30       -7.51\par   Baht             36.01          32.90       -8.64\par   Peso             46.83          44.72       -4.51\par   Rupiah        14265.00       12380.00      -13.21\par   Rupee            66.34          63.03       -4.98\par   Ringgit         4.3210         3.4965      -19.08\par   Yuan            