\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  firm ahead of Fed meeting, inflows buoy Taiwan dollar, won\b0\par\par\uc2 831 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2015\par 08:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Taiwan dollar, won near 6-month highs on foreign demand\par     * S.Korea, Taiwan c.banks suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, April 28 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose on Tuesday as the dollar remained broadly weak\par amid optimism over Greece's debt deal, while caution grew ahead\par of the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy meeting.\par     The Taiwan dollar and the South Korean won\par  hit near six-month highs on demand linked to recent\par stock inflows.\par     The won hovered around a seven-year peak to the yen\par  after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Monday said it has downgraded\par Japan's credit rating. \par     China's yuan rebounded after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set\par a stronger midpoint and as traders suspected major state-run\par banks sold the dollar for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     The dollar stayed weaker against a basket of six\par major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with the euro's strength based on renewed hopes\par that debt-laden Greece stood a better chance of securing fresh\par funding after Prime Minister Alexis Tsipras reshuffled his team\par handling talks with international lenders. \par     The dollar's weakness came ahead of the two-day meeting of\par the Federal Open Market Committee which starts later in the\par session, though the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to alter its\par policy stance as recent economic data have been disappointing,\par analysts said.\par     "It looks to me like people got stung the last time with all\par the USD longs before March FOMC. Now it gets the other way,"\par said Andy Ji, Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of\par Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore, referring to the Federal Open Market\par Committee.\par     "If the Fed just keeps the hikes on the table this year, we\par will see people buying USD back."\par     Last month, the Fed downgraded its economic growth and\par inflation forecasts, causing investors to dump dollar holdings,\par which they had built up on expectations of an early interest\par rate hike by the Fed.\par     That also lifted most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as low U.S.\par borrowing costs help the region maintain appeals of higher\par yields.\par     \par     TAIWAN DOLLAR\par     The Taiwan dollar rose 1.3 percent to 30.472 per U.S.\par dollar, its strongest since Nov. 3 on inflows from foreign\par financial institutions and exporters' month-end demand.\par     The island's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could strengthen to 30.200 if stock\par inflows continue, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader in Taipei said.\par     Foreign investors in the previous session bought a net\par T$21.6 billion ($707.0 million) worth of local stocks,\par bring their net equity purchases for the month so far to T$80.5\par billion, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taiwan Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  data showed.\par     The island's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pared some of earlier gains as the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  around 30.500 to stem\par strength in the best performing Asian unit this year, traders\par said.\par     Importers were also buying dollars to meet payments.\par     \par     WON\par     The won gained as much as 0.4 percent to 1,069.0 per dollar,\par its strongest since Oct. 31, on demand from offshore funds.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities were spotted\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the won's strength, traders said, especially\par as it stayed around a seven-year high to the yen.\par     The won on Monday hit 8.9596 to the yen, its strongest since\par February 2008.\par     The two countries compete in overseas markets, exporting\par products like electronics and cars.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit advanced 0.7 percent to 3.5440 per dollar, its\par strongest since Feb. 9, tracking its appreciation in overnight\par non-deliverable forwards markets.\par     Most government bond prices also gained, while traders\par expected oil exporters to buy the ringgit for month-end\par settlements.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0600 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.06         119.03       -0.03\par   Sing dlr        1.3271         1.3281       +0.08\par   Taiwan dlr      30.527         30.861       +1.09\par   Korean won     1069.90        1073.00       +0.29\par   Baht             32.59          32.69       +0.32\par   Peso             44.26          44.28       +0.05\par   Rupiah        12983.00       12975.00       -0.06\par   Rupee            63.30          6