\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories of the Day\b0\par\par\uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2015\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA'S CONSUMER INFLATION INCHES UP IN MARCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China posted another modest rise in consumer inflation in March, giving policy makers room for more aggressive stimulus for a sluggish economy. (published 0318 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN SAYS IT IS PURSUING BALANCED FISCAL, MONETARY POLICIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the face of fresh U.S. criticism that Japan is too reliant on monetary policy and too hasty to rebuild its finances, the nation's finance minister responded Friday by saying that Tokyo is pursuing a balanced approach to growth and budgetary challenges. (published 0024 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par U.S. CHIDES EUROPE, JAPAN FOR OVERRELIANCE ON MONETARY POLICY -- 2ND UPDATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Obama administration chastised Europe and Japan for excessive reliance on monetary policy to revive stagnant growth, worried that a failure to deploy other policy tools could further undermine an already gloomy global economic outlook. (published 2349 GMT Thursday) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MODI ARRIVES IN FRANCE FOR BOAT TRIP ON THE SEINE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indian Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Narendra Modi\f28 \uc2 \f2  arrived in Paris on Thursday evening on the start of his three-nation tour that includes a visit to Germany and Canada. (published 0516 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA ECONOMY IN 'TARGETED RANGE,' NO DEFLATION SEEN - ECONOMIST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economy has been facing downward pressure but hasn't slipped out of its targeted growth range, a senior economist said Friday. (published 0504 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MALAYSIA FEBRUARY INDUSTRIAL PRODUCTION RISES 5.2% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's industrial production climbed 5.2% in February from a year earlier, slightly above market expectations, thanks to strong gains in mining output, official data Friday showed. (published 0400 GMT) \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SOUTH KOREA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DENIES \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean officials Friday denied the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury Department\f28 \uc2 \f2 's fresh report on Seoul's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. (published 0248 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN HOUSING FINANCE INVESTMENT SLOWS IN FEBRUARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The value of lending to Australian property investors slowed sharply in February easing concerns about overheating in the housing market. (published 0156 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINES JANUARY NET FOREIGN DIRECT INVESTMENT $263 MLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign direct investments in the Philippines showed a net inflow of $263 million in January, helped mainly by funds placed by foreign companies in debt securities issued by their local affiliates, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Friday. (published 0552 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par REPORT SLAMS DIGITAL HEALTH RECORDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Obama administration took vendors of electronic health records to task for making it costly and cumbersome to share patient information and frustrating a $30 billion push to use digital records to improve quality and cut costs. (published 0400 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OBAMA SIGNALS CUBA'S REMOVAL FROM TERRORISM LIST -- 2ND UPDATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barack Obama\f28 \uc2 \f2  is nearing an expected decision to remove Cuba from the U.S. list of governments that sponsor terrorism, a step that would jettison a key hurdle toward normalizing relations between the two countries. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 10, 2015 02:00 ET (06:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150410eb4a000gz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
