\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories of the Day\b0\par\par\uc2 276 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2015\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA MARCH CPI +1.4% ON YEAR; MATCHING EXPECTATIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's consumer inflation held steady in March, something that should give the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more scope for further policy easing, official data showed Friday. (published 0146 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN SAYS IT IS PURSUING BALANCED FISCAL, MONETARY POLICIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the face of fresh U.S. criticism that Japan is too reliant on monetary policy and too hasty to rebuild its finances, the nation's finance minister responded Friday by saying that Tokyo is pursuing a balanced approach to growth and budgetary challenges. (published 0024 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par U.S. CHIDES EUROPE, JAPAN FOR OVERRELIANCE ON MONETARY POLICY -- 2ND UPDATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Obama administration chastised Europe and Japan for excessive reliance on monetary policy to revive stagnant growth, worried that a failure to deploy other policy tools could further undermine an already gloomy global economic outlook. (published 2349 GMT Thursday) \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SOUTH KOREA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DENIES \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean officials Friday denied the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury Department\f28 \uc2 \f2 's fresh report on Seoul's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. (published 0248 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN HOUSING FINANCE INVESTMENT SLOWS IN FEBRUARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The value of lending to Australian property investors slowed sharply in February easing concerns about overheating in the housing market. (published 0156 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OBAMA SIGNALS CUBA'S REMOVAL FROM TERRORISM LIST -- 2ND UPDATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barack Obama\f28 \uc2 \f2  is nearing an expected decision to remove Cuba from the U.S. list of governments that sponsor terrorism, a step that would jettison a key hurdle toward normalizing relations between the two countries. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 09, 2015 23:00 ET (03:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150410eb4a0005j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
