\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Dismal U.S. jobs data lift Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; ringgit at five-week high\b0\par\par\uc2 793 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2015\par 07:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Ringgit up on higher oil prices, short-covering, fixing\par     * Won near ten-week high on offshore funds\par     * Rupiah around two-week peak on firm NDFs\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, April 6 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rallied on Monday as disappointing U.S. jobs data\par added to expectations that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  may delay an\par interest rate hike and help the region maintain yield\par attraction.\par     The ringgit hit a near five-week peak as higher oil\par futures eased concerns over the outlook for the current account\par and fiscal deficit in Malaysia, an oil and gas exporter. Oil\par futures rose after Saudi Arabia raised prices for crude sales to\par Asia. \par     South Korea's won rose to an almost ten-week high\par and Indonesia's rupiah advanced to its strongest in\par near two weeks.\par     U.S. non-farm payrolls rose by 126,000 in March, the\par smallest gain since December 2013, data showed on Friday, well\par below economists' forecasts for a rise of 245,000.\par  The data pushed down \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields and\par the dollar.\par     "The shocking jobs data accelerated the dollar's\par corrections. That will support Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more for the\par short term," said Jeong My-young, Samsung Futures' research head\par in Seoul.\par     "Still, it is still possible for the Fed to raise interest\par rates within this year as U.S. data may improve in the second\par quarter. So, Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are unlikely to appreciate in the\par long run."\par     Investors now expect the Fed to raise rates in September,\par rather than June, as the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lowered forecasts for\par  economic growth and inflation at its March policy meeting.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit jumped 1.4 percent to 3.6205 per dollar, its\par strongest since March 3, as investors scrambled to cover short\par positions in the second worst-performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far\par this year.\par     The ringgit garnered more support from demand at a daily\par fixing and as investors bought it against the neighbouring\par Singapore dollar.\par     Long-term Malaysian government bond prices rose, with the\par 10-year yield down to 3.841 percent, the lowest\par since March 9.\par     The Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strengthened past chart resistance\par lines at 3.6372, a 55-day moving average, and 3.6355, the 50\par percent Fibonacci retracement of its depreciation from February\par to March.\par     It may rise to 3.6110, the 61.8 percent retracement, if the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ends the day firmer than the 50 percent level, analysts\par said.\par     \par     WON\par     The won rose as much as 0.9 percent to 1,082.5 per dollar,\par its strongest since Jan. 28, on demand from offshore funds.\par     Caution grew over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities to stem the won's strength.\par     The won hovered near a seven-year high against the yen\par , raising concerns that South Korea's exporters may\par lose price competitiveness against Japanese rivals.\par     \par     RUPIAH\par     The rupiah advanced as much as 0.6 percent to 12,920 per\par dollar, its strongest since March 25, tracking its overall\par strength in non-deliverable forwards markets.\par     The official Jakarta Interbank Spot Dollar Rate,\par which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  introduced in 2013 to manage exchange\par rate fluctuations, was fixed at 12,942 rupiah per dollar,\par stronger than the previous 12,995.\par     The Indonesian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pared some of earlier gains on\par dollar demand from local companies.\par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0450 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.02         118.93       -0.08\par   Sing dlr        1.3527         1.3505       -0.16\par  *Taiwan dlr      31.303         31.303       +0.00\par   Korean won     1083.80        1092.70       +0.82\par   Baht             32.50          32.50       +0.02\par   Peso             44.38          44.58       +0.46\par   Rupiah        12935.00       13000.00       +0.50\par   Rupee            62.16          62.50       +0.54\par   Ringgit         3.6265         3.6700       +1.20\par  *Yuan            6.1950         6.1950       +0.00\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.02         119.66       +0.53\par   Sing dlr        1.3527         1.3260       -1.97\par   Taiwan dlr      31.303         31.718       +1.33\par   Korean won     1083.80        1099.30       +1.43\par   Baht             32.50          32.90       +1.25\par   Peso             