\par\par \b \uc2 The Morning Ledger: Could Obamacare Be Undone?\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Willhite   \par \uc2 2,678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 12:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Morning Ledger from CFO Journal cues up the most important news in corporate finance every weekday morning. Send us tips, suggestions and complaints: james.willhite@wsj.com. Get The Morning Ledger emailed to you each weekday morning by clicking {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/TheMorningLedgerSignup"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/TheMorningLedgerSignup}}}\uc2  . Follow us on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @CFOJournal.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. The Supreme Court will hear oral arguments on Wednesday in King v. Burwell, the latest challenge to the Affordable Care Act, the WSJ's Anna Wilde Mathews reports. If conservatives have their way, the court will decide that a few words in the law mean that federal subsidies to consumers in 37 states are unlawful, and effectively unravel marketplaces there. Such a decision could knock Obamacare into a "death spiral" of a shrinking pool of insured Americans and higher premiums for those who keep coverage through federal exchanges in those states.\par \par \uc2 But such a move is far from certain, and even if the court does rule against the federal subsidies that make plans in the affected states affordable to lower-income residents, Obamacare requirements on businesses will remain the law of the land--even if some commentators hope that such a ruling would be the beginning of the end for the law.\par \par \uc2 The case has put a cloud of uncertainty over insurers that are putting the final touches on their products and prices for 2016. "We're hedging our bets right now," says Ferris W. Taylor, chief strategy officer of Utah's Arches Health Plan. That insurer may propose an array of product designs this spring, then drop some of them before they're finalized, depending on what the court decides.\par \par \uc2 CFO JOURNAL TODAY\par \par \uc2 Almost half of global audits have problems. Nearly half of global audits inspected last year were deficient in some way, an international accounting consortium said Tuesday, CFO Journal's Maxwell Murphy reports. Top concerns included internal controls testing, measuring fair value of assets, and revenue recognition, according to the International Forum of Independent Audit Regulators. The organization examined almost 950 reports from 29 countries.\par \par \uc2 THE DAY AHEAD\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ADP\uc2  private-employment report for February releases at 8:15 a.m., and analysts are expecting 220,000 new jobs, up somewhat from January's 213,000. Then, at 2 p.m., the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  releases its Beige Book of economic activity, giving investors a peek at a key indicator ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 Smells like teen dispirit. Investors are bracing for more teen angst when \uc2 Abercrombie & Fitch Co\uc2 . and \uc2 American Eagle Outfitters Inc\uc2 . report fiscal full-year results on Wednesday, Ahead of the Tape's Spencer Jakab reports. The apparel retailers have suffered an almost unbroken string of sales declines lately.\par \par \uc2 CORPORATE NEWS\par \par \uc2 Tech firms buy up property. Some of Silicon Valley's biggest companies are on a real-estate buying binge, paying premium prices to make sure they have enough space for future expansion. \uc2 Facebook Inc\uc2 . agreed last month to spend $395 million for Menlo Science and Technology Park, a jumble of 21 low-slung warehouses and office buildings 30 miles southeast of San Francisco. \uc2 Google Inc\uc2 . spent more than $1 billion last year on at least 19 properties in Silicon Valley. The purchases, known as land banking, are being done in part to give the companies breathing room as they add employees. But it also a hedge against future real-estate price increases and helps fend off rivals from planting flags in their backyards.\par \par \uc2 GE CEO \uc2 Jeff Immelt\uc2 's crude problem. \uc2 General Electric Co\uc2 .'s CEO already had problems convincing investors he was moving fast enough to remake the conglomerate into a simpler industrial machine, and now there's this oil business. Mr. Immelt spent $14 billion on companies that help oil and gas drillers pump and transport. \uc2 GE\uc2  was touting the business as recently as September, but Mr. Immelt's annual investor meeting in December included a warning on oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  rewards shareholders. \uc2 Best Buy Co\uc2 . unveiled plans to buy back shares for the first time since 2012 and boosted its dividend by 21%, as the electronics retailer posted better-than-expected profit in its holiday quarter. The chain said it would repurchase $1 billion in shares over three years as part of an existing $5 billion authorization. It also announced a 21% increase to its quarterly dividend and a special dividend of 51 cents a share.\par \par \uc2 Actavis\uc2  sells $21 billion of bonds. \uc2 Actavis PLC\uc2  sold the second-biggest corporate-bond offering in history, with a $21 billion deal fueled by investors' desire for returns amid low interest rates. The sale comes as highly rated firms this year are selling debt at the fastest pace on record.\par \par \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  and BlackRock are raising their voices. Vanguard Group and \uc2 BlackRock Inc\uc2 ., giant fund managers once known for quiet relationships with the companies in which they invest, say they are getting more assertive. \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  is urging boards to be "substantially independent of management," and BlackRock, in newly revised voting guidelines, signaled for the first time that it may oppose U.S. board members' re-election.\par \par \uc2 Citi deal shrinks the bank's umbrella. \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 .'s move to sell its U.S. subprime-lending business to \uc2 Springleaf Holdings Inc\uc2 . is emblematic of postcrisis regulations forcing banks out of their old mind-set of trying to serve as many customers as possible under one roof.\par \par \uc2 J.P. Morgan treasurer to step down. \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase & Co\uc2 .'s treasurer, \uc2 Craig Delany\uc2 , is planning to leave the bank but will stay on until a successor is onboard. Mr. Delaney, who is in his mid-40s, will retire in the coming months to spend more time with his family, people familiar with the matter said. He also oversees the bank's Chief Investment Office, a role he assumed following the "London whale" trading fiasco.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  hit by \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  charge. \uc2 Barclays PLC\uc2  raised its provision for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlements to $1.92 billion, leading it to report a full-year net loss, but said an effort to shrink and restructure its business is starting to pay off. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  cut around 5% of its workforce last year. More than 2,000 of the cuts were in its investment bank, and a further 5,000 of the remaining 20,500 investment-banking jobs are set to go over the next two years.\par \par \uc2 The next marketing frontier: your medical records. \uc2 Practice Fusion Inc\uc2 . is pioneering a new type of data-driven health care. Its software alerts doctors when a medical record indicates that a patient needs a particular treatment. Some of those alerts are sponsored by drug companies. \uc2 Practice Fusion\uc2  gives its software to clinics free of charge. It crunches 100 million patient records it has stored remotely in an online database to alert providers when treatments or tests might be needed. Some worry the sponsored alerts blur the line between promoting health and marketing medicines.\par \par \uc2 Investors balk at deals for specialty retailers. The recent collapse of private-equity buyouts of \uc2 Chico's FAS Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Express Inc\uc2 . signal that investors and lenders are growing increasingly wary of specialty retailers. The sector has seen a rash of bankruptcies, and banks have been hit by losses as they struggled to resell loans backing earlier deals.\par \par \uc2 The driverless car, officially, is a risk. Three insurance companies and an auto-parts maker warned in their most recent annual reports that driverless cars and the technology behind them could one day disrupt the way they do business. The investor warnings aren't meant to spell out an imminent threat. But their inclusion in the catch all "Risk Factors" section of corporate filings indicates that driverless cars are now close enough to reality that companies feel they need to flag the risks to their investors.\par \par \uc2 SUV sales plow through February. Poor weather modestly dented the pace of U.S. auto sales in February, but did little to stem America's increasing thirst for pricey trucks and SUVs. Meanwhile, old-school auto makers are gearing up to take on the threat posed by emerging competitors \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . in a technological and financial gunfight.\par \par \uc2 Target to cut several thousand jobs. \uc2 Target Corp\uc2 . plans to lay off several thousand employees at its Minneapolis headquarters and elsewhere as CEO \uc2 Brian Cornell\uc2  looks to get the retailer back on track and shoppers back in its stores.\par \par \uc2 For tech's highfliers, Fab is a cautionary tale. \uc2 Fab.com\uc2  had early traction and blue-ribbon backers, but instead of becoming a billion-dollar e-commerce juggernaut, it's a cautionary tale for startups. PCH, an Irish electronics contract manufacturer, on Tuesday said it had acquired the \uc2 Fab.com\uc2  website and two offices in New York and India. Fab co-founder \uc2 Jason Goldberg\uc2  and PCH founder \uc2 Liam Casey\uc2  declined to comment on the final price, but people with knowledge of the matter said the two sides were negotiating for a price in the low tens of millions. Fab was valued at close to $1 billion two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Sharp to ask lenders for new support. \uc2 Sharp Corp\uc2 .'s president plans to meet with the troubled electronics company's two main lenders to ask for a new round of financial support, less than three years after its last bailout. A turnaround plan is expected to be presented to the core banking units of \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group Inc\uc2 ., which together hold more than $3.3 billion in Sharp debt.\par \par \uc2 Fraud comes to \uc2 Apple Pay\uc2  . A payments expert tells the WSJ that it's not an anomaly to see fraud accounting for about 6% of \uc2 Apple Pay\uc2  transactions, compared to about 0.1% of transactions using a plastic card to swipe. The reason for the high number lies with some banks that make it too easy for customers to be approved because they wanted to reduce the friction of adding their cards to \uc2 Apple Pay\uc2 . An Apple spokeswoman says \uc2 Apple Pay\uc2  is "designed to be extremely secure and protect a user's personal information." She added that "banks are always reviewing and improving their approval process, which varies by bank."\par \par \uc2 4 Mar 2015 06:39 ET  The Morning Ledger: Could Obamacare Be Undone? -2-\par \par \uc2 Samsung\uc2  bets on its chips in Galaxy S6 . The Exynos chips powering \uc2 Samsung Electronic Co\uc2 .'s new Galaxy S6 phones are the product of a multiyear effort to improve the company's chip design and manufacturing process. Since 2010, \uc2 Samsung\uc2  has been spending at least $10 billion each year to upgrade and expand its chip facilities, including plants that make memory chips. The investment frees from having to rely on \uc2 Qualcomm Inc\uc2 . chips for its devices and, in the long run, help boost \uc2 Samsung\uc2 's logic-chip division.\par \par \uc2 NBCU plans to launch comedy video service. \uc2 Comcast Corp\uc2 .'s NBCUniversal is aiming to launch a comedy-focused subscription Web video service later this year, as interest grows in reaching young online viewers who don't watch traditional TV. NBCU is considering various possible price points--one range under considerations is $2.50 to $3.50 a month. It is the latest media giant to venture beyond the cable-TV bundle to target people who are "cutting the cord," as well as "cord nevers" who have never paid for traditional TV.\par \par \uc2 REGULATION\par \par \uc2 Yellen scolds Wall Street on culture, ethics. Federal Reserve Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  raised concerns about the state of Wall Street's culture and ethics, echoing similar comments from other regulatory officials and increasing pressure on the biggest U.S. banks to improve.\par \par \uc2 Crackdown deals Macau a rough hand. Macau gambling revenue remained in a free fall in February, sinking 49% from a year earlier amid China's crackdown on corruption. The drop marks the ninth-straight month of shrinking revenue in the Chinese territory. February's slide eclipsed a previous record drop in December, when it fell 30%.\par \par \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  settles robo-signing charges. A \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase & Co\uc2 . unit struck a $50 million deal with regulators who accused the bank of filing "robo-signed" mortgage documents to bankruptcy courts across the country. The bank will make payments to more than 25,000 homeowners, including some who weren't properly notified that their mortgage payments increased after they filed for bankruptcy protection.\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 Inflation in developed economies halved in January. The annual rate of inflation across the world's developed economies more than halved in January, reaching its lowest level since the recession that followed the global financial crisis. That widespread disinflation is likely to prompt further cuts in benchmark interest rates or other easing measures by central banks around the world.\par \par \uc2 Seoul swallows tough medicine for economy. Government's push for telemedicine, now available only on remote islands, is a test case for a broader effort to reduce the country's dependence on exports by developing its weak service industries. Economists see the process as essential for \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long-term prospects.\par \par \uc2 Denmark \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in a big way. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a record amount of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month in a bid to protect the krone's peg to the euro, underscoring the fallout from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to boost economic growth.\par \par \uc2 CFO MOVES\par \par \uc2 KKR Financial Holdings LLC\uc2 , which New York-based financial-services company \uc2 KKR & Co. LP\uc2  acquired last year, named Thomas Murphy chief financial officer, according to a regulatory filing. He succeeds Michael McFerran, who resigned.\par \par \uc2 FLIR Systems Inc\uc2 ., a Wilsonville, Ore.-based sensor-systems company, named \uc2 Dave Muessle\uc2  interim CFO. Mr. Muessle is vice president, corporate controller and principal accounting officer at the company. CFO \uc2 Tony Trunzo\uc2  resigned effective March 27. Mr. Trunzo received compensation valued at $3.4 million in 2013, according to a proxy filing.\par \par \uc2 Brinker International Inc\uc2 ., a Dallas-based casual-dining restaurant company, named Thomas Edwards Jr. CFO, effective March 16. Mr. Edwards was CFO at Wyndham Hotel Group. He will succeed Guy Constant, who left the company last March and whose duties have since been handled by interim CFO \uc2 Marie Perry\uc2 , the company's treasurer and controller, a company spokeswoman told CFO Journal. Mr. Constant received compensation valued at $2.6 million during the company's 2014 fiscal year, according to a proxy filing.\par \par \uc2 Herman Miller Inc\uc2 ., a Zeeland, Mich.-based interior-furnishings company, named \uc2 Jeff Stutz\uc2  CFO. Mr. Stutz was treasurer and chief accounting officer at the company. He succeeds \uc2 Greg Bylsma\uc2 , whom the company made chief operating officer for a North American division, a company spokesman told CFO Journal. Mr. Bylsma received compensation valued at $1.1 million during the company's fiscal year ended in 2014, according to a proxy filing. \par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 Barclays Plc\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=GB0031348658"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=GB0031348658}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 Sharp Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=JP3359600008"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=JP3359600008}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 Apple, Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US0378331005"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US0378331005}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 Barclays Plc\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US06738E2046"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US06738E2046}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 Best Buy Co., Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US0865161014"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.companyspotlight.com/partner?cp_code=P479&isin=US0865161014}}}\par \par \uc2 Access Investor Kit for \uc2 BlackRock, Inc\uc2 .\pa