\par\par \b \uc2 German GDP Shines Brighter Than Anticipated -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2015\par 08:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   0728 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--German GDP comes in better than expected, up 0.7% on the quarter and 1.4% on the year. There was "considerably more" capital spending, particularly on equipment, machinery and construction, versus the third quarter, the German statistics agency said. In an on-year comparison, household consumption was a "notable" support, it added. Analysts had a expected a 0.3% rise on the quarter and an increase of 1.0% versus the fourth quarter of 2013. For all of 2014, the economy expanded 1.6%, slightly better than the agency's preliminary estimate of 1.5% in January.  (nikki.houston@wsj.com) \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par   0727 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Hope that the Ukraine ceasefire will hold and Monday's Eurogroup meeting On Greece will have more success than Wednesday's effort kept the euro firm overnight. Thursday's poor U.S. retail sales continued to weigh on the greenback with uncertainty over future BoJ policy, a poor JGB auction and a 0.4% fall in the Nikkei also taking its toll on USD/JPY. Up ahead EMU 4Q GDP is the morning data highlight, already out French 4Q GDP expanded just 0.1% as expected while Germany grew by 0.7% vs 0.3% forecast and lifted EUR/USD to a fresh 1-week high of 1.1440. gary.stride@wsj.com \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par 0725 GMT  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) Bangkok residents are expected to spend about 6 billion baht ($183.4 million) during Lunar New Year this month, Kasikorn Research estimates. The projected spending represents a 3.5% growth compared to 3.6% in 2014. "Most spenders are still very careful due to household debt conditions and high living costs that pressure their purchasing power," Says Kasikorn. "The country's economy recovery is still unclear, and so the average spending per household will probably be around 3,750 baht [$114.6]." Thailand's inflation in January contracted 0.41% but the cost of living was still very high because the price of consumer goods had not yet factored in falling oil prices, it says. (wilawan.watcharasakwet@wsj.com) \par Editor JSM \par 0718 GMT  [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's economy is expected to grow 5.1%, TA Securities forecasts. The country on Thursday reported 5.8% on-year economic growth for the October-to-December quarter. The economy grew 5.6% on-year during the third quarter and for the full-year 2014 the growth rate stood at 6%. "Looking ahead, we maintain our 2015 GDP growth forecast of 5.1%, which is in-line with the government's guidance of 4.5% to 5.5%, due to anticipated softening in domestic demand" post implementation of a consumption tax later this year. TA Securities says that lower global crude oil and crude palm oil prices and weakness in China's economy will further dampen Malaysia's economic growth prospects.(saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; Twitter: @journosaurabh) \par Editor: DMF \par 0712 GMT  [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Recently, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not excessively \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to devalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Brian Aitken, head of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Korea mission. "The won should be allowed to respond flexibly to market forces whether that is appreciation or depreciation," Mr. Aitken told a press briefing after his policy consultation with Korean officials on Friday. "We haven't seen any signs of sustained one-way \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a recent period." The \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  last year publicly questioned South Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy and told Seoul to refrain from any excessive market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . South Korean policy makers have insisted that their "smoothing" action be only limited to reducing market volatility. Asian economies often devalue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to make their exporters' goods cheaper in overseas markets. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \par Editor: DMF \par   0711 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--We're off to a pretty feeble start, with France's statistics agency recording a 0.1% increase in gross domestic product over the final three months of the year. In the whole of 2014, the economy expanded by just 0.4%, matching the weak 2013 performance. In other words, the French economy continues to stagnate, and since it is the second-largest member of the eurozone, that's a problem for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area as a whole.The paralysis is most apparent in the country's lackluster consumer spending, which is supposed to be the engine of France's economy. One bright spot in the French data: Exports. A weakening euro helped lift exports by 2.3% in the final three months of the year. paul.hannon@wsj.com \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par 0701 GMT  [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's economy is expected to grow 5.2%, AmResearch forecasts. The country on Thursday reported 5.8% on-year economic growth for the October-to-December quarter. The economy grew 5.6% on-year during the third quarter and for the full-year 2014 the growth rate stood at 6%. "Going forward, we anticipate a slower growth for 2015 as domestic demand is expected to soften. Our full-year gross domestic product projection is 5.2% for 2015," it says in a note. It says that risks ahead for the Malaysian economy include a slowdown in loan growth due to stringent lending practices by financial institutions and weaker consumer sentiment; fiscal consolidation and the introduction of consumption tax later this year. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; Twitter: @journosaurabh) \par Editor JSM \par   0700 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The very weak end to the year for the French economy is a reminder as to why President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Francois Hollande\f28 \uc2 \f2  was so keen for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to launch a program of quantitative easing that he announced it three days before ECB President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a minor breach of protocol. The ECB in turn believes QE alone won't help boost growth and lift inflation unless political leaders like Mr. Hollande drive through potentially unpopular reforms to labor and product markets. But among the many German objections to QE is the argument that by boosting growth even in the short run, it will ease the pressure on Mr. Hollande and his fellow leaders to be aggressive. On this tension hangs the fate of the eurozone economy in 2015 and beyond. paul.hannon@wsj.com \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par 0658 GMT  [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the outlook for China's port sector remains constructive despite concerns about an on-going shift of production from China. The house cites continued economic recovery in the U.S., China's free-trade pacts with other countries, and a possible tariff hike as favourable factors in supporting the earnings outlook and sentiment for China's port stocks in 2015. "Our economists expect US GDP to grow at an above-trend 3% in 2015 after four consecutive years of weaker-than-expected growth, as consumption spending catches up with the acceleration in real income growth," it says. The house forecasts an average of 13% earnings growth for the sector in 2015 compared with 10% in 2014E. It adds that a plan by China to develop a "Maritime Silk Road" would likely facilitate trade. The ports along the "Maritime Silk Road" including those in Southeast China, Colombo and Piraeus in Athens, may benefit from increased trade activities in the long run, it says. (joanne.chiu@wsj.com, Twitter:@joannechiuhk) \par Editor: DWT \par 0649 GMT  [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong retail sales are softening and the weaker euro is partly to blame -- because it makes buying handbags in Europe cheaper, say \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts who play the role of fashion buyers in their latest report. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  finds that French and Italian luxury handbags are on average 37% cheaper in their home countries than in Hong Kong. If a shopping trip to Europe sounds a bit too far away, Japan could be a closer alternative for big spenders in China. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  finds that buying luxury handbags in Japan is around 9-21% cheaper than in Hong Kong, thanks to the weaker yen. Both the euro and yen have fallen around 14% versus the Hong Kong dollar in the past six months. (jacky.wong@wsj.com; Twitter: @jackycwong) \par Editor: DWT \par 0642 GMT  [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Olam is bullish on coffee and cocoa as the company is expecting supply deficits, says chief executive Sunny Verghese at company's fourth-quarter results briefing in Singapore. Coffee prices have been supported by dry weather in major grower in Brazil while global cocoa demand is fast-growing. Olam expects Brazil's new coffee crop this year to be at 49 million bags--lower than the company's previous estimate of 51 million bags. The deficit for the Arabica grade is forecasted at four to five million bags this year and five to six million bags next year, Verghese says; "as the market begins to realize the tightness, we will see coffee price rallying further." For cocoa, major growing regions in Africa are experiencing extremely dry winds from the Sahara desert, and this will likely impact the Ghana cocoa crop, which may drop to 750,000-760,000 tons this year from 960,000 tons last year, he says. Meanwhile, Olam is bearish on rice prices due to the Thai government's efforts to sell the country's large rice stock. (huileng.tan@wsj.com) \par Editor JSM \par 0638 GMT  [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Mobile Internet traffic is on the rise in India and ecommerce companies are stepping up hiring and raising more funds to tackle this expansion, says Jefferies. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Internet and Mobile Association of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects the number of mobile Int-ernet users in India to exceed 200 million by June 2015, Jefferies says. This is in line with feedback from companies noting a sharp surge in mobile traffic over the past 12 to 18 months, it says. Even at 200 million, mobile Internet penetration would still be only 28% of the total number of mobile subscribers, says Jefferies, adding that there is "significant growth potential even from here".(dhanya.thoppil@wsj.com) \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Editor: DWT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13-02-15 0742GMT\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150213eb2d0007z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
