\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ringgit leads Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  losses in 2014, another bumpy year seen\b0\par\par\uc2 830 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2014\par 05:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Ringgit down 6.3 pct in 2014 on sliding oil prices\par     * Won, Taiwan dollar fall on yen worries\par     * Baht is 2014 pest performing Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SEOUL, Dec 31 (Reuters) - Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell in\par 2014, led by the Malaysian ringgit, and regional units are\par expected to stay under pressure in the new year due to\par expectations the U.S. Federal Reserve will hike interest rates\par and China's economy will stay sluggish.\par     Regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday rose as the yen \par extended gains with investors taking profits ahead of the\par year-end.\par     The daily gains were not enough to prompt hopes of rebounds\par in emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2015 as a solid U.S. economy is\par likely to drive the Fed to raise interest rates.\par     China's economy continues to slow down. Activity in the\par country's factory sector shrank for the first time in seven\par months in December, according to a private survey.\par  \par     "Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are seen weakening further next year,\par given possibilities of U.S. rate hikes," said Jeong My-young,\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung\f28 \uc2 \f2  Futures' research head in Seoul.\par     "Southeast Asian units may suffer more as they are more\par sensitive to the Chinese economy and oil prices, while Northeast\par Asia peers may find some support from the U.S. recovery," Jeong\par added.\par     The dollar was on course to end 2014 with a 12 percent gain\par against a basket of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , its largest advance since\par 2005.\par     The greenback is seen strengthening further as a U.S. rate\par hike will boost its appeal when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are easing monetary policies.\par     \par     OIL HURTS RINGGIT\par     The ringgit was set to be the worst performing\par emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of 2014 with a 6.3 percent loss\par against the dollar, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par     GRAPHIC on Asia-Pacific \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  performance\par                               \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/geh73w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/geh73w}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par     As Malaysia is a net oil exporter, plunging prices slapped\par the ringgit on concerns they will hurt the country's current\par account and widen its fiscal deficit. Oil prices fell to 5-1/2\par year lows .\par     "I reckon the ringgit will weaken slower only if oil prices\par move back to $75-85," said a senior Malaysian bank trader in\par Kuala Lumpur. On Wednesday, both Brent and U.S. crude stayed\par well below $60 per barrel.\par     Northeast Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - South Korea's won \par and Taiwan's dollar - fell on worries that a sliding\par yen may hurt export competitiveness.\par     This year, the Taiwan dollar has fallen 5.2\par percent, which would be its biggest annual loss since 2001.\par     The won ended this year with a near 4 percent depreciation,\par the largest annual slide since the 2008 global financial crisis.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities have been spotted\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to check the won's strength against the yen\par . The won rose to its strongest since 2008 against the\par Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The Singapore dollar has lost 4.4 percent this year\par on slowing economic growth and inflation.\par     China's yuan has slid 2.4 percent to see its\par first meaningful annual depreciation since the landmark\par revaluation in 2005, due to the slowdown hitting the world's\par second-largest economy.\par     The Indian rupee and the Indonesian rupiah\par , the worst performing emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par 2013, both have eased more than 2 percent this year. Those\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell less than most regional peers on hopes of\par economic reforms by their new governments.\par     Thailand's baht was the best performing Asian unit\par of 2014, with only fractional change, as inflows were attracted\par after the army seized power in May to end months of political\par turmoil.    \par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0445 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.42         119.48       +0.05\par   Sing dlr        1.3208         1.3216       +0.06\par   Taiwan dlr      31.586         31.766       +0.57\par   Korean won     1091.00        1099.30       