\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Firm dollar hurts Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , yuan weakest since June\b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2014\par 06:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Yuan nearly touches 2-pct daily limit for first time\par     * Won down on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  caution\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SEOUL, Dec 30 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par eased on Tuesday in the face of broad U.S. dollar strength, with\par China's yuan hitting its weakest in more than six months.\par     The yuan fell as much as 0.2 percent to 6.2362\par per dollar, its weakest since June, and almost touched its\par 2-percent trading limit for the first time, on growing bearish\par sentiment towards the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's won eased on caution over possible\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities to check its\par strength against the yen, traders said.\par     The Malaysian ringgit hovered near a five-year low\par as the dollar hit a 29-month high to the euro on worries that a\par snap election in Greece may derail the country's international\par bailout.\par     The political uncertainty hurt risk appetite, dragging\par Asia-Pacific stocks outside Japan lower.\par     "The dollar is a king. The game is to be long the USD," said\par a senior Malaysian bank trader in Kuala Lumpur, adding emerging\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are weaken further next year.\par     Regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have lost ground so far this year\par partially due to expectations that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  may raise\par interest rates in 2015, prompting investors to withdraw funds\par from emerging markets and channel them back to the United\par States.\par     Trading was thin across Asia ahead of the New Year's holiday\par and as many investors have already closed out their positions\par for 2014.\par     \par     WON\par     The won slid as investors were wary of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday hit 9.0944 against the yen, its\par strongest since March 2008.\par     South Korea and Japan compete in overseas markets for sales\par of cars, ships and electronics.\par     The won pared some of earlier losses on demand from\par exporters for year-end settlements.\par     South Korea's current account surplus rose to a seasonally\par adjusted $9.73 billion in November, its highest in seven months,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data showed earlier. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering Co Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par also said it has won a combined 2.2 trillion won ($2.0 billion)\par orders to build ships, prompting expectations of more demand for\par the won. \par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0450 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       120.40         120.65       +0.21\par   Sing dlr        1.3246         1.3240       -0.05\par   Taiwan dlr      31.731         31.761       +0.09\par   Korean won     1098.90        1097.80       -0.10\par   Baht             32.95        