\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Strong dollar hurts Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; ringgit near 5-year low despite oil rebound\b0\par\par\uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2014\par 06:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Malaysia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected to cut ringgit slides\par     * Rupiah hits lowest since 2008 global crisis\par     * Won falls as yen hits 7-year low\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Dec 3 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par skidded lower on Wednesday as the dollar climbed to a seven-year\par high against the yen, buoyed by higher \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields on a\par more bullish outlook for the world's top economy.\par     Malaysia's ringgit hit a near five-year low as a\par rebound in oil failed to ease concerns over the impact of lower\par crude prices on the country, a net oil exporter and major palm\par oil producer.\par     The Indonesian rupiah touched 12,298 per dollar,\par its weakest since December 2008, amid the global financial\par crisis, on local corporate dollar demand.\par     South Korea's won tracked the yen's slide. \par     The dollar index, a gauge of the greenback's\par performance against six major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , stayed around 4-1/2-\par year high, on stronger U.S. bond yields.\par     The dollar was supported by comments earlier this week from\par New York Fed President \f28 \uc2 \f2 William Dudley\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Vice Chair Stanley\par Fischer who both provided a mostly rosy outlook for the U.S.\par economy. \par     "Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will stay weaker as everybody agrees on a strong\par dollar trend," said Jeong My-young, Samsung Futures' research\par head in Seoul.\par     "We may see some profit-taking from the dollar, but it is\par hard to reverse the trend unless U.S. economic data is bad or\par other economies show a solid picture of health."\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit lost as much as 0.5 percent to 3.4420 per\par dollar, its weakest since February 2010. Kuala Lumpur stocks\par  lost 0.8 percent, well underperforming other Southeast\par Asian peers.\par     The Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pared some of its earlier losses as\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to limit its\par downside, traders said.\par     Oil rebounded more than 1 percent with U.S. crude for\par January rising to $67.97 a barrel earlier in the day, but\par investors stayed concerned about Malaysia's weakening growth due\par to lower oil prices.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Malaysia's economic growth in 2015 would\par be lower than the bank's forecast of 4.8 percent and next year's\par budget deficit target of 3 percent could be more difficult to\par achieve if oil price were to stay at $70.\par     "At current oil price levels, we think MYR could depreciate\par past 3.55-3.60 before it represents a bigger risk to BNM's\par inflation outlook," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a client note,\par referring to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     \par     WON\par     The won fell as the yen's weakness increased caution over\par possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par authorities to stem the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength against the Japanese\par unit.\par     Against the yen, the won advanced to 9.3231, a notch weaker\par than a six-year high of 9.3116 hit on Nov. 28.\par     The won pared some of its losses against the dollar as\par exporters bought it on dips for settlements.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0435 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.21         119.21       +0.00\par   Sing dlr        1.3104         1.3115       +0.08\par   Taiwan dlr      31.118         31.106       -0.04\par   Korean won     1113.10        1106.80       -0.57\par   Baht             32.91          32.87       -0.12\par   Peso             44.75          44.74       -0.02\par   Rupiah        12293.00       12267.00       -0.21\par   Rupee            61.93          61.88       -0.07\par   Ringgit         3.4390         3.4240       -0.44\par   Yuan            6.1500         6.1503       +0.00\par  \par   Change so far in 2014\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.21         105.28      -11.69\par   Sing dlr        1.3104         1.2632       -3.60\par   Taiwan dlr      31.118         29.950       -3.75\par   Korean won     1113.10        1055.40       -5.18\par   Baht             32.91          32.86       -0.15\par   Peso             44.75          44.40       -0.78\par   Rupiah        12293.00       12160.00       -1.08\par   Rupee            61.93          61.80       -0.20\par   Ringgit         3.4390         3.2755       -4.75\par   Yuan            6.1500         6.0539       -1.56\par     \par \par  (Additional reporting by IFR Markets' Catherine Tan; Ed