\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ringgit near 5-yr low as oil tumbles; Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  down in Nov\b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2014\par 09:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Updates prices. For midday report, double-click\par  )\par     SINGAPORE, Nov 28 (Reuters) - Malaysia's ringgit fell to a\par near five-year low on Friday as oil prices tumbled, while\par emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were poised to suffer depreciation in\par November.\par     The ringgit lost as much as 0.8 percent to 3.3750\par per dollar, its weakest since March 2010, as offshore funds\par dumped the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on fears that lower oil prices may hurt\par Malaysia, a net oil exporter and major palm oil producer.\par     The country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to limit\par the ringgit's losses, traders said.\par     The South Korean won ended November down 3.6\par percent against the dollar, reporting a third consecutive\par monthly depreciation. That was the longest monthly losing streak\par since the November 2008 global credit crisis, Thomson Reuters\par data shows.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities were spotted\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to check the won's strength against the yen\par , traders said. The won hit 9.3116 on Friday to the\par Japanese unit, its strongest since August 2008.\par     The ringgit has lost 2.5 percent so far in November, which\par would also be a third straight monthly slide. The Taiwan dollar\par  closed the month 1.8 percent weaker.\par     Singapore's dollar has fallen 1.3 percent, which\par would be a fifth consecutive monthly depreciation, the longest\par monthly losing spree since March 2013.\par     The Indian rupee and the Indonesian rupiah \par have lost 1 percent each.\par     The Chinese yuan has fallen 0.5 percent.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0800 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       118.23         117.79       -0.37\par   Sing dlr        1.3035         1.2980       -0.42\par   Taiwan dlr      31.030         30.951       -0.25\par   Korean won     1107.04        1098.40       -0.78\par   Baht             32.80          32.78       -0.06\par   Peso             44.89          44.85       -0.09\par   Rupiah        12198.00       12176.00       -0.18\par   Rupee            61.99          61.88       -0.19\par   Ringgit         3.3750         3.3465       -0.84\par   Yuan            6.1450         6.1392       -0.09\par  \par   Change so far in 2014\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       118.23         105.28      -10.96\par   Sing dlr        1.3035         1.2632       -3.09\par   Taiwan dlr      31.030         29.950       -3.48\par   Korean won     1107.04        1055.40       -4.66\par   Baht             32.80          32.86       +0.18\par   Peso             44.89          44.40       -1.10\par   Rupiah        12198.00       12160.00       -0.31\par   Rupee            61.99          61.80       -0.31\par   Ringgit         3.3750         3.2755       -2.95\par   Yuan            6.1450         6.0539       -1.48\par     \par \par  (Reporting by Jongwoo Cheon; Editing by Prateek Chatterjee)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-