\par\par \b \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Meeting in Focus for USD/JPY -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2014\par 02:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The \uc2 OPEC\uc2  meeting later today will be crucial to provide clues regarding future crude oil prices especially before the year-end big shopping season in the U.S., says \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Yunosuke Ikeda\uc2  in a morning note. \uc2 OPEC\uc2  members are facing an impasse to iron out differences in opinions and remain cautious about output reduction until getting a detailed understanding about U.S. shale oil supplies. Mr. Ikeda says whether WTI oil prices fall to $70 for now (now at $72.60) will be closely monitored. Lower crude oil prices will work its way through to a higher USD/JPY by pushing up the U.S. economy and \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yields. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor SXS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Australia's equities market is in positive territory despite weaker energy and mining shares, echoing the small gains seen on Wall Street overnight. The \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 is ahead 0.2% at 5404.3, helped by gains in banks. NAB (NAB.AU) is up 1.3% while Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU), \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) and ANZ (ANZ.AU) are up between 0.1% and 0.3%. Tristan K'Nell, head of trading at Quay Equities, notes the market bounced higher after the release of capital expenditure data which should offer some comfort to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that some businesses outside mining will increase spending into next year. Despite a further fall in the price of iron ore, Fortescue (FMG.AU) is 1.1% higher, but \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  (BHP.AU) is down 1% and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) is off 0.2%. \uc2 Woolworths\uc2  (WOW.AU) is 1.4% stronger after the chairman affirmed profit guidance for the fiscal year. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @RobbMStewart) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0127 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Philippine peso rises to 44.90 versus the U.S. dollar, sending USD/PHP below the October trendline support at 44.91. The break of this technical resistance paves the way to the Bollinger downtrend channel at 44.88. A confirmation of the downtrend channel coming into play--contingent on a Thursday close below 44.880--would weigh the dollar further. The market maybe capitulating on long-USD positions after U.S. economic data released overnight failed to meet expectations. Another possible catalyst for peso strength could be the Philippines' third-quarter gross domestic product data due at 0200 GMT. Upbeat trade figures released Tuesday bode well for the GDP report. Analysts forecast the economy grew 6.5% on-year in the third quarter versus the 6.4% growth in the second quarter. USD/PHP is now 44.90 from its Wednesday close of 44.93. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0124 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Better-than-expected export figures doesn't much alter the economic outlook for Thailand, with Citi predicting that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dovish bias will likely persist. Shipments increased 4% on-year in October, which meant the country recorded a trade surplus, thanks in large part to low oil prices. Citi reckons a sluggish start to the government's fiscal year 2015 spending "could threaten domestic demand gains", and may prompt the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  to trim interest rates when its monetary policy committee meets next week. (wilawan.watcharasakwet@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0121 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/SGD has lost its bullish technical bias due to Wednesday's bearish closing. The pair is now likely to slip toward the 20-day Bollinger mid support line at 1.2952. Data released in the U.S. on Wednesday depressed the U.S. dollar. A higher than expected number of jobless claims, weaker new home sales, and tepid inflation were factors that weighed on the greenback. Singapore's industrial output for October rose 0.2% on-year, sharply lower than forecasts for a rise of 2.1%. The local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was however unaffected by the data and continues to be guided by external factors instead. USD/SGD is now 1.2981 from its Wednesday close of 1.2971. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0116 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW has fallen out of the Bollinger uptrend channel and could soon test the round-figure trading barrier at 1,100--a break of which could trigger stoploss orders that might weigh the greenback further. USD/KRW might find a stronger support at 1,095 where the 20-day Bollinger mid support line awaits. But as the won rallies versus the U.S. dollar, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cannot be ruled out. The South Korean won is still close to its strongest versus the yen since 2008, a factor that could influence \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the USD/KRW spot market to weaken the won. A stronger won versus the yen makes South Korea exports' less competitively-priced versus those of Japan. USD/KRW is now 1,101.7 from its Wednesday close of 1,106.5. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0114 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RIO) says it will pour more cash into one mine it thinks is an absolute gem--its Diavik diamond mine. Executives say the move--which comprises the US$350 million development of a new pipe at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  it runs with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dominion Diamond Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .--shows it's committed to and confident in the diamond-mining industry that it unsuccessfully tried to exit last year. Diamond prices reached a record in the summer of 2011, but have largely fallen since. "Our decision to invest in the Diavik A21 project reflects our strong confidence in the diamond sector and in our ability to compete effectively in the industry," says Rio diamonds and minerals Chief Executive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alan Davies\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The Polished Prices index opened this week 0.2% lower than the week prior, at 141.7, but sits roughly around the same level as a year ago. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @RhiannonHoyle) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0110 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/GBP is likely to trade with risks skewed to the downside Thursday. The daily chart is tilting negative as a bearish outside-day-range pattern was completed Wednesday, the slow stochastic measure is bearish, while the MACD line is staging a bearish crossover against its exponential moving average. Support is at 0.7893 (Wednesday's low, near the 55-day moving average); a breach would target 0.7878 (Nov. 13 low), then 0.7794 (Nov. 12 low). Resistance is at 0.7950 (Wednesday's high); a breach would temper the negative near-term view, exposing upside to 0.8002 (Friday's high). Spot EUR/GBP is at 0.7918. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0108 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] AUD/USD is likely to consolidate in a higher range Thursday after hitting a four-year low 0.8477 on Wednesday. The pair is supported by weaker USD sentiment and an unexpected 0.2% increase in Australia 3Q capital spending (versus forecast for 1.9% fall). But AUD/USD gains are tempered by weak iron ore prices (which fell $0.60 Wednesday to more-than-five-year low of $68.00/ton). The daily chart is mixed as the MACD and slow stochastic indicators are bearish, while the five- and 15-day moving averages are declining; but a bullish hammer candlestick pattern completed Wednesday. Resistance is at 0.8565 (Wednesday's high); a breach would expose upside to 0.8619 (Tuesday's high). A drop below 0.8477 would reinstate the negative near-term view, exposing downside to 0.8314 (July 1, 2010 reaction low). Spot AUD/USD is at 0.8553. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0102 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  ]EUR/JPY is likely to consolidate Thursday. The daily chart is mixed as the MACD indicator is bullish but the slow stochastic measure is bearish near overbought levels. Resistance is at 147.41 (Wednesday's high); a breach would expose upside to 148.43 (Friday's high), then to 149.12 (six-year high hit Nov. 20). Support is at 146.51 (Wednesday's low); a breach would turn the near-term view negative, targeting 146.31 (Tuesday's low), then 145.59 (Monday's low). Spot EUR/JPY is at 147.08. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0101 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CAD is likely to trade in a lower range Thursday, undermined by soft USD sentiment. But USD/CAD losses are tempered by weak prices of oil, Canada largest export (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  crude fell to a low of $73.30/bbl Wednesday, just shy of the four-year low of $73.25 hit on Nov. 14). The daily chart is negative-biased as the MACD indicator is bearish, the five-day moving average is below the 15-day moving average and declining, while the slow stochastic measure is staying suppressed at oversold levels. Support is at 1.1221 (Monday's low, near the 55-day moving average); a breach would target 1.1189 (Friday's low), then 1.1177 (Oct. 31 low). Resistance is at 1.1297 (Wednesday's high); a breach would temper the negative near-term view, targeting the 1.1316-1.1326 band (Tuesday's high-Friday's high). Spot USD/CAD is at 1.1249. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0055 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  ]USD/CHF is likely to trade in lower range Thursday, weighed by weaker USD sentiment after a slew of disappointing U.S. economic data overnight. The daily chart is mixed as the MACD indicator is bearish but the slow stochastic measure is neutral. Support is at 0.9593 (Wednesday's low); a breach would expose downside to 0.9564 (Friday's low), then to 0.9550 (Nov. 20 low) and 0.9530 (Nov. 19 low, near the 55-day moving average). Resistance is at 0.9666 (Wednesday's high); a breach would tilt the near-term view positive, targeting 0.9699 (Tuesday's high). Spot USD/CHF is at 0.9610. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 26, 2014 20:47 ET (01:47 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141127eabr0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
